// TEST: Range SymIdx Named Alias Carries The Component Tag
// Was "Range SymIdx Named Alias Rejects", pinning a hard BL4003 as OBSERVED
// BEHAVIOR and saying so: naming the SymIdx group turned a warning into a
// refusal with no semantic difference between the two programs. That is now
// fixed, so this file pins the fix instead of the symptom.
//
// An index record has ONE `Tag` field for the whole group (IRIndexTypeG.Tag,
// "name (index space matching)"), and `elemTypeForIterationIndex` hands that
// tag to EVERY component param. The nominative-alias rule used to overwrite it
// with the alias name, so for `type S2 = SymIdx<2, I3>` both params typed as
// `Nat<S2>` -- the GROUP's name, a type no component index of S2 can inhabit --
// and indexing the I3-tagged array they actually range over was BL4003 at ERROR
// severity. A multi-rank record now keeps its COMPONENT space's tag, which is
// docs/formalism.md 7.3's stated minimal fix.
//
// THE ASSERTION IS SILENCE. There is no `// WARN:` pin here, and warning pins
// are strict in BOTH directions, so any warning this file emits fails it. That
// is what proves the params are `Nat<I3>` and nothing weaker: untagged `Int64`
// params would warn BL4003 twice (that is 174, which still does, because its
// group is anonymous and has no component tag to inherit), and `Nat<S2>` params
// would error. Only the component tag passes silently.
//
// The values are 172's and 174's, unchanged -- the tag fix moves no data.
type I3 = Idx<3>
type S2 = SymIdx<2, I3>
let v: Array<Float like I3> = [2.0, 3.0, 5.0]
let sym = method_for(range<S2>) <@> lambda(p0, p1) -> v(p0) * v(p0 + p1) |> compute
// EXPECT: sym = [[4, 6, 10], [9, 15], [25]]

// The trade the fix makes explicit: a multi-rank alias names the class for
// readability but mints no distinct nominal identity, exactly as the wreath
// carve-out beside it already did. So an array annotated with the ALIAS and one
// annotated with the group written INLINE are the same type, and this
// assignment is legal rather than a nominal mismatch.
let inline_spelled: Array<Float like SymIdx<2, I3>> = sym
// EXPECT: inline_spelled = [[4, 6, 10], [9, 15], [25]]
